editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Keep candidate indices in increasing index order. For minima, pop larger or equal values from the back, since the new value is no larger and expires later. For maxima do the opposite. Remove expired indices from the front; the remaining fronts are the extrema.

## Walk through the sample
For [3,1,1], the newer 1 can replace the older 1 as a minimum candidate because it expires later. Shifting to [1,1,7] keeps the minimum 1 but moves the maximum to 7.

## Complexity
Time O(n), space O(k) besides input or output storage.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, k;
    cin >> n >> k;
    deque<pair<int, int>> low, high;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        if (!low.empty() && low.front().first <= i - k)
            low.pop_front();
        if (!high.empty() && high.front().first <= i - k)
            high.pop_front();
        while (!low.empty() && low.back().second >= x)
            low.pop_back();
        while (!high.empty() && high.back().second <= x)
            high.pop_back();
        low.emplace_back(i, x);
        high.emplace_back(i, x);
        if (i >= k - 1)
            cout << low.front().second << ' ' << high.front().second << '\n';
    }
}
